          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 22-year-old hostel resident presents with a dry cough, low-grade fever, and bi</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 22-year-old hostel resident presents with a dry cough, low-grade fever, and bilateral diffuse infiltrates on chest X-ray. He continues attending classes despite his symptoms. Which organism is most likely responsible?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Streptococcus pneumoniae</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. S. pneumoniae typically causes lobar consolidation with rusty sputum and acute illness, not bilateral diffuse infiltrates with mild symptoms.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Klebsiella pneumoniae</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Klebsiella causes severe necrotising pneumonia typically in alcoholics and diabetics, with currant-jelly sputum — not a mild illness in a young adult.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pseudomonas aeruginosa</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Pseudomonas is associated with hospital-acquired pneumonia and cystic fibrosis, not community-acquired disease in healthy young adults.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mycoplasma pneumoniae</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! Mycoplasma pneumoniae is the classic cause of 'walking pneumonia' in young adults (5–40 years), characterised by mild symptoms and bilateral diffuse infiltrates.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is Mycoplasma pneumoniae. In the 5–40 age group, Mycoplasma is the leading cause of atypical 'walking' pneumonia — the patient remains ambulatory despite bilateral infiltrates."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right! Mycoplasma pneumoniae lacks a cell wall and causes the mild 'walking pneumonia' classically seen in young adults living in close quarters like hostels."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
