          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 45-year-old man has a cavitary lesion in the right lung apex, while an HIV-pos</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 45-year-old man has a cavitary lesion in the right lung apex, while an HIV-positive woman presents with diffuse bilateral miliary shadows and hepatosplenomegaly. Which statement best explains the pathogenetic difference between these two forms of tuberculosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Both result from haematogenous spread but differ only in bacterial load</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Reactivation TB does not involve haematogenous spread — it arises from dormant bacilli reactivating locally at the apex. Only miliary TB is haematogenous.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The first results from local reactivation at a high-oxygen site with caseation and cavitation, while the second results from bloodstream dissemination seeding multiple organs</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! Reactivation TB favours the oxygen-rich apex, causing localised caseation and cavity formation. Miliary TB spreads through the bloodstream, seeding tiny granulomas across the lungs, liver, spleen and meninges.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The first is a primary infection while the second is reactivation disease</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. An apical cavitary lesion is characteristic of reactivation (post-primary) TB, not primary infection. Primary TB forms the Ghon complex in the lower lung fields.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The first is caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis while the second is caused by Mycobacterium avium complex</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. Both forms are caused by M. tuberculosis. M. avium complex causes disseminated disease in severely immunocompromised patients but does not produce the classic miliary TB pattern.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Reactivation TB arises from dormant bacilli at the oxygen-rich lung apex, causing localised caseation and cavitation. Miliary TB, by contrast, disseminates through the bloodstream, producing widespread granulomas across multiple organs."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent analysis! Reactivation TB reactivates locally at the high-oxygen apex, while miliary TB disseminates haematogenously — two fundamentally different pathogenetic mechanisms producing very different clinical pictures."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
