          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A child presents with a greyish-white pseudomembrane on the tonsils that bleeds </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A child presents with a greyish-white pseudomembrane on the tonsils that bleeds when dislodged. A throat swab smear is prepared. Which staining technique will demonstrate the characteristic metachromatic granules of the suspected organism?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Gram stain</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. A Gram stain would show Gram-positive bacilli but does not reveal the metachromatic (volutin) granules that are the hallmark of Corynebacterium diphtheriae.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Albert stain</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct! Albert stain demonstrates the characteristic blue-black metachromatic granules (volutin granules) within yellowish-green bacilli of Corynebacterium diphtheriae.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ziehl-Neelsen stain</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. ZN stain detects acid-fast bacilli such as Mycobacterium tuberculosis. It has no role in diagnosing diphtheria.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Potassium hydroxide mount</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Incorrect. A KOH mount dissolves cellular material to reveal fungal elements such as Candida pseudohyphae — it is not used for bacterial infections.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is Albert stain. Remember the three throat swab stains: Gram stain screens for bacteria generally, Albert stain specifically targets C. diphtheriae granules, and KOH mount reveals fungal elements."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! When a pseudomembrane raises suspicion for diphtheria, Albert stain is the specific technique to demonstrate the diagnostic blue-black metachromatic granules of C. diphtheriae."
                }
              ],
